  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m The request names a new module this repo doesn't have, and there's a real fork
  underneath it — the browser side has no module system at all, so "put config in a module" isn't one obvious edit. I'll
  ask a couple of questions, propose approaches, and write a short spec before any code. Say the word if you'd rather I
  treat it as a quick bounded change and just present a two-paragraph design instead.

  First question — what "easier to change environments" has to actually do:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether switching dev → staging → prod means [3mediting a value[0m or [3mselecting a pre-declared environment[0m. This
  one sets the settings module's shape and is the expensive one to change later; everything else (file naming, where
  it's imported) is cheap.

  - [1mEdit one constant[0m — settings.js holds a single [38;5;153mapiEndpoint[39m. Simplest possible, but switching environments is still a
    hand-edit of a URL, so it barely improves on today. Rules out having dev/prod URLs both visible in the repo.
  - [1mEnvironment map + one switch[0m — settings.js declares [38;5;153mdevelopment[39m/[38;5;153mstaging[39m/[38;5;153mproduction[39m blocks and a single [38;5;153mENV[39m constant
    selects one. Switching is a one-word change, all URLs are documented in one place. Costs you a decision about what
    the environment names are.
  - [1mAuto-detect from hostname[0m — no manual switch at all; [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m gets dev, everything else gets prod. Zero-touch
    deploys, but the mapping is implicit and harder to override when you want prod config locally. Can be layered on the
    map later, so it's not a one-way door.

  [1mMy recommendation: environment map + one switch.[0m It's the option that actually delivers the stated goal ("easier to
  change environments") without inventing infrastructure this repo has no tooling for, and hostname auto-detect can be
  added on top of it later without reshaping the module.
